# Argentum backend configuration. Copy to .env and fill in.
#
# One deployment serves every tenant: companies are rows in the control
# database, created through signup, and each one registers its own sources.
# The "one App Instance = one tenant" header this file used to carry, and the
# TENANT_ID / TENANT_NAME / SOURCE_DB_* / INTERNAL_DB_* variables under it,
# described the architecture before the multi-tenant refactor — **nothing in
# the tree has read any of them since**, while the two variables the process
# genuinely refuses to boot without were absent. A template that cannot boot
# the process it is the template for is how a fresh machine loses an
# afternoon; found by the §1q gate sitting on 2026-08-22, which lost one.

# Runtime
ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
PORT=8080

# Control-plane Postgres — owned by this deployment. Chat history, agents,
# sources, audit, jobs. Migrated automatically on boot; `make infra` runs one
# locally on 5432 with these defaults.
#
# A tenant's *analytics* database is not configured here. Sources are
# registered per company through the dashboard and stored encrypted in
# `db_connections` — there is no environment variable for one, and there has
# not been since the multi-tenant refactor.
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
DB_USER=argentum
DB_PASSWORD=argentum123
DB_NAME=argentum
DB_SSL_MODE=disable

# Where the control migrations are read from. The default resolves from the
# process directory, an ancestor, or the module root, so it is only worth
# setting in a container that copies them somewhere else.
# CONTROL_MIGRATIONS_DIR=migrations/control

# Redis — the queue the worker consumes and the schema cache. `make infra`
# exposes it on 6380 to stay out of the way of a system Redis on 6379.
REDIS_URL=localhost:6380

# --- The two secrets the process will not boot without ---------------------
#
# Both are checked by config.Validate(), so an empty value is exit 1 with the
# variable named rather than a subtle failure later. Generate them:
#
#   ARGENTUM_JWT_SECRET:  openssl rand -base64 48
#   ARGENTUM_DSN_KEY:     openssl rand -hex 32     <- exactly 64 hex chars
#
# ARGENTUM_DSN_KEY seals every stored source DSN and every tenant LLM
# credential. **Losing it loses those rows** — they cannot be recovered from a
# backup of the database alone. Changing it is a rotation, not an edit: see
# ARGENTUM_DSN_KEYS_RETIRED below.
ARGENTUM_JWT_SECRET=
ARGENTUM_DSN_KEY=

# Keys that are still accepted for *reads* and never used for writes (T-H14).
# Comma-separated hex, same format as the primary.
#
# This is the variable that turns a key change from a hard cutover into a
# window: set the new key as ARGENTUM_DSN_KEY, the old one here, deploy, then
# `go run ./cmd/rekey -check` / `-apply` / `-check`, and only then remove this
# line and deploy again. Every step before the last is reversible. The full
# procedure is in cmd/rekey's package comment.
# ARGENTUM_DSN_KEYS_RETIRED=

# LLM — primary agent
LLM_PROVIDER=gemini
LLM_API_KEY=
LLM_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
# Light LLM — used for guardrails / classifier / summary. Leave LIGHT_LLM_API_KEY
# empty to reuse the primary client.
LIGHT_LLM_PROVIDER=gemini
LIGHT_LLM_API_KEY=
LIGHT_LLM_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash-lite
# Zero Data Retention. When true, every inference request through an OpenRouter
# base URL carries provider.zdr, so it may only be routed to endpoints that
# retain nothing and cannot train on the payload. Off by default because it
# narrows the endpoint pool: a model with no ZDR endpoint returns 404 rather
# than falling back. Check https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/endpoints/zdr covers
# LLM_MODEL and LIGHT_LLM_MODEL before turning it on. Ignored (with a startup
# warning) for non-OpenRouter hosts and for the anthropic/gemini interfaces.
LLM_ZDR=false

# WhatsApp pipeline. Optional — leave the whole block empty and the channel is
# simply off. Nothing here can stop the process from starting.
#
# ENABLE_WHATSAPP is read by nothing — no Go file references it (checked
# 2026-08-11). `/webhook/whatsapp` is mounted on every deployment either way;
# with no secret configured it answers 401, so it is inert rather than open.
#
# Leaving this block half-filled logs a warning naming the variable that is
# missing. That is the only case worth saying anything about: nothing set is a
# choice, and some of it set is a mistake.
#
# WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET is the key `/webhook/whatsapp` verifies the Meta
# signature with, and that signature is the endpoint's only authentication —
# the route is public by design, because Meta holds no Argentum credential.
# Unset, every callback is answered 401 — so inbound WhatsApp is off, not open —
# and ENV=production logs a warning at boot rather than refusing to start
# (T-H1/T-H3; the boot refusal was reverted 2026-08-11 because a config check
# that stops the process turns a security fix into a rollout outage).
# WHATSAPP_PROVIDER=twilio signs with TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN instead, which the
# Twilio block below already requires.
ENABLE_WHATSAPP=false
# WHATSAPP_PROVIDER=whatsapp_business
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
# WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
# WHATSAPP_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN=
# RABBITMQ_URL=amqp://admin:admin123@rabbitmq:5672/

# Object storage (MinIO / S3-compatible). Everything the product hands back as
# a file lives here: generate_document, /v1/reports, and the tenant logo on a
# branded PDF. Leave MINIO_ENDPOINT empty and those paths degrade rather than
# fail — the tool is not registered and /v1/reports/render answers
# `rendering_unavailable` — which is a hard thing to debug if you did not mean
# it.
#
# `make infra` runs one locally on 9000 (console on 9001) with these defaults.
# The bucket is created by whichever process starts first, so there is nothing
# to provision by hand.
MINIO_ENDPOINT=localhost:9000
MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=minioadmin
MINIO_SECRET_KEY=minioadmin
MINIO_BUCKET=argentum-documents
MINIO_USE_SSL=false

# A hosted S3-compatible provider is the same five variables with different
# values — no code path differs, and this is how the other Go services in this
# workspace are deployed (IDCloudHost, `is3.cloudhost.id`):
#
#   MINIO_ENDPOINT=is3.cloudhost.id
#   MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=…
#   MINIO_SECRET_KEY=…
#   MINIO_BUCKET=argentum
#   MINIO_USE_SSL=true          <- required; the endpoint is https-only
#
# Two things to know before pointing a deployment at a shared account. The
# credentials are account-wide, not bucket-scoped, so a key that can write
# `argentum` can read every other bucket on it — mint a separate one if the
# provider allows it. And downloads stay presigned and time-limited whatever
# the endpoint is: a generated report is a tenant's numbers, and an object
# served public-read is served to anyone who ever saw the link.

# Metabase (optional)
METABASE_URL=http://metabase:3000
METABASE_ADMIN_EMAIL=
METABASE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# App tuning
CONTEXT_MAX_TURNS=3
HISTORY_HYDRATE_LIMIT=20
MAX_QUERIES_PER_MINUTE=10

# When a metric returns exactly 0, spend two more queries — the same metric over
# everything before the window and everything after it — to find out whether the
# window is inside the data at all.
#
# It is on because the alternative was measured: a metric template is written
# COALESCE(SUM(x), 0), so a window the data does not reach comes back as a
# genuine 0 rather than as NULL, and both models in the 2026-08-14 matrix run
# answered "Rp 0" for a quarter ending after the warehouse does. The cost is two
# queries on a zero and nothing at all on every other answer.
METRIC_ZERO_COVERAGE_PROBE=true

# After the agent answers, ask it what is worth asking next (T-Q10). The
# suggestions ride the assistant message's metadata and the `final` event, and
# the dashboard draws them as chips under the newest answer.
#
# One light-model call per answered turn, in front of the `final` event — so it
# costs money and it costs latency the browser waits through. Setting this false
# restores the previous turn exactly: no chips, no call, and a persisted message
# byte-identical to what it was. The pass is skipped anyway on a turn that asked
# a clarifying question, on a reply a guardrail replaced, on a report, on a
# schedule or watcher fire, and on a tenant near its credit floor.
#
# DEFAULT FALSE since 2026-08-17, because the gate measured it: 607 µUSD per
# pass (≈3% of the turn) and 12,962 ms, against T-Q10's own rule of "revisit
# above 1s". On by default, a deployment like the one that measured this pays
# NEXT_STEPS_TIMEOUT_SECS of latency on every answer and gets no chips at all.
# Turn it on once your light tier returns three short suggestions in about a
# second — a reasoning model will not, whatever its price.
NEXT_STEPS_ENABLED=false

# How long that pass may take, in seconds.
#
# 8 is a compromise rather than a measurement. T-Q10 specified 5s; the first live
# turn (2026-08-17) measured this deployment's light model — gpt-5-nano through
# OpenRouter — at 12-17s for the pass, so the feature was switched on and timed
# out on every turn, billing nothing and doing nothing. Covering that by default
# would put 15 seconds in front of every answer, which the ticket's own rule
# (revisit above 1s) forbids.
#
# So: raise it if your light model is fast, and watch for the Warn line that says
# the pass never completes. A reasoning model is the wrong choice here — most of
# those 13 seconds were reasoning tokens for three short suggestions.
NEXT_STEPS_TIMEOUT_SECS=8

# Per-turn agent budget (T-16). Four dimensions, all enforced per chat turn;
# whichever runs out first ends the turn with an explicit incomplete answer
# rather than a fabricated figure. Lowering these does not make the agent
# cheaper so much as blunter — it answers with less evidence.
AGENT_MAX_ITERATIONS=8
AGENT_MAX_TOOL_CALLS=12
AGENT_MAX_TURN_TOKENS=200000
AGENT_TURN_BUDGET_SECS=150

# Credit enforcement (T-03). The balance is checked before a turn is queued on
# every channel and on every scheduled fire; a company at or below zero is
# refused with a 402 and a plain sentence, and nothing is spent.
#
# CREDITS_DEFAULT_GRANT_USD is what a company with no grant is provisioned on
# its first check. It is not optional decoration: nothing else in the system
# ever credits a company, so with a grant of 0 and enforcement on, every tenant
# on platform keys is refused. It is a starting balance, not a monthly refill —
# nothing tops it back up.
#
# A tenant with their own primary key in company_llm_credentials is never
# refused, whatever the balance says. Set ENABLED=false to restore the
# pre-T-03 behaviour: still metered, never refused.
CREDITS_ENFORCEMENT_ENABLED=true
CREDITS_WARNING_THRESHOLD_PCT=20
CREDITS_DEFAULT_GRANT_USD=25

# Public API (T-13). Requests to /v1 authenticate with an API key minted in
# Settings -> API keys, not with a dashboard session.
#
# API_V1_RATE_PER_MIN is a per-key budget in its own Redis bucket, separate
# from the per-company dashboard limiter: a tenant's nightly job and a tenant's
# staff should not be able to exhaust each other. The bucket refills at
# rate/60 per second and bursts to the full minute's budget.
API_V1_RATE_PER_MIN=120

# The kill switch (T-A1). False answers 503 on every /v1 route including
# /v1/me, above authentication, and leaves /api untouched — the dashboard
# keeps working while the public surface is off the air.
API_V1_ENABLED=true

# Caps the synchronous door on /v1/chat (T-A3). A turn runs five to seven tool
# calls; past this the caller gets a resumable 504 carrying the thread id
# rather than a dead connection. Do not raise it when someone complains —
# point them at the SSE stream.
API_V1_SYNC_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120

# Bounds a /v1 request body. A report spec arriving over HTTP is untrusted in
# a way the agent's own spec never was: refusing to read it is cheaper and
# safer than validating it afterwards.
API_V1_MAX_BODY_BYTES=1048576

# What a posted spec may ask the renderer to lay out (T-A2). The body cap above
# is not enough on its own: 50 000 rows of small integers fit comfortably inside
# a megabyte, and maroto will attempt every one of them. Rows are counted across
# the whole document, not per table.
API_V1_MAX_SPEC_ROWS=50000
API_V1_MAX_SPEC_COLS=40

# How long POST /v1/reports/render holds a connection open before the render
# becomes a job and the caller gets a 202. Distinct from
# API_V1_SYNC_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, which is a chat turn: a render that takes twenty
# seconds is pathological, and a chat turn that takes twenty seconds is Tuesday.
API_V1_SYNC_RENDER_TIMEOUT=20

# --- The video renderer (T-V3) ------------------------------------------------
#
# Where apps/render answers. **Empty is a supported deployment**: `mp4` is
# refused with a plain sentence, it is left out of generate_document's format
# enum entirely, and every other format is untouched. `make infra` starts one on
# :8090, so uncomment the first line to produce a video on a developer machine.
#
# Both the API and the worker read it, and they need it for different reasons:
# the worker runs the render, and the API only has to know the format exists so
# it can answer 202 rather than "unavailable".
# RENDER_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8090
# RENDER_SHARED_SECRET=
#
# Bounds. RENDER_TIMEOUT_SECS sits above the render service's own ten-minute
# wall clock, so a service that gives up answers us rather than being cut off
# and reported as unreachable. The scene and frame caps are checked before a
# job is queued: what they protect is minutes of a render pod that does one job
# at a time, and the agent can spend those as easily as an integrator can.
# RENDER_TIMEOUT_SECS=900
# RENDER_MAX_SCENES=60
# RENDER_MAX_FRAMES=18000

# Permits a report callback_url pointing at an address only this deployment can
# reach — loopback, RFC1918, link-local — and permits plain http. Leave false
# anywhere it matters: the URL is chosen by the caller, and a POST to
# 169.254.169.254 on their behalf is how an outbound webhook becomes somebody
# else's cloud credentials. True is for local development.
API_V1_CALLBACK_ALLOW_PRIVATE=false

# How often the `/v1` request recorder writes what it has buffered (T-A5). It is
# the staleness of the tenant's own error list traded against the write rate of
# the rollup; the acceptance criterion is "within a minute". A killed process
# loses at most one interval — a clean shutdown flushes.
API_V1_OBS_FLUSH_SECONDS=15

# How long `/v1` request counters and failures are kept. Longer than any
# debugging session, short enough that the tables stay small. Pruned at most
# hourly by whichever API process is running.
API_V1_OBS_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# When the worker enforces every tenant's `message_retention_days` (T-H6).
# Nightly: the work is a bulk DELETE against the tables live turns write to,
# and a window measured in days does not need twenty-four lock windows a day.
#
# **Empty switches it off**, which means a tenant who set a retention window in
# the dashboard has one stored and not applied. The worker logs a Warn saying
# exactly that on every boot, because a retention promise that is silently not
# kept is worse than one that was never offered.
RETENTION_PURGE_CRON=41 3 * * *

# Gates `/metrics`: `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
#
# Set    — the token, or 401. An authorized scrape also gets the per-key block,
#          which labels request counters by API key id.
# Unset  — loopback only, without the per-key block. Everyone else gets 404.
#
# Anything that scrapes across the network — Prometheus, a sidecar, a curl from
# your laptop against a deployed pod — needs this set. The snapshot carries this
# deployment's LLM spend, token totals and query volumes, which is why an
# endpoint that cannot authenticate anybody answers to the local machine only.
# An empty value is never a match. Prometheus exposition format and an internal
# listener are still T-17.
METRICS_TOKEN=

# Where cmd/mcp listens (T-14). Argentum's own tools, served over MCP so a
# customer's agent — Claude Code, or one they wrote — can call them with an API
# key. A separate process on a separate port: an MCP session is long-lived, and
# one that hangs must not exhaust the dashboard's connection budget.
#
# The surface is the read tools plus the two Metabase writes, each gated on a
# scope; docs/mcp/setup.md is the client-side page.
MCP_SERVER_ADDR=:8081

# OpenTelemetry tracing (T-17). Unset means no provider is installed and every
# span is a no-op — the behaviour this product had before the package existed —
# so instrumenting the turn path costs nothing on a deployment with no
# collector.
#
# The variable names are OTel's own rather than Argentum's, so a deployment
# already exporting from another service needs no setting specific to us.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL is grpc (default) or http/protobuf.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4317
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=grpc
#
# Locally, the collector is the compose file's `jaeger` service, behind the
# `tracing` profile so an ordinary `docker compose up` still starts nothing:
#
#   docker compose --profile tracing up -d jaeger
#
# then set the two below and restart api + worker. Both processes must export
# or the waterfall is half a trace — the turn span starts on the worker.
# Waterfalls at http://localhost:16686.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://jaeger:4318
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf
